There exist a finite abelian group $\mathcal{Y}$, a constant $c>0$, and a function $\delta\colon\mathbb{N}\to(0,1]$ satisfying $\delta(d)\ge d^{-c}$ for all $d\ge 1$, such that for every $d\in\mathbb{N}$ and every $N\in\mathbb{N}$ the following holds.

Let $\mathbf{F}$ and $\mathbf{G}$ be any two probability distributions over functions $\mathcal{Y}^N\to\mathbb{C}$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{unit $\ell_2$ norm:} $\lVert f\rVert_2 = 1$ for every $f\in\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{F})$ and $\lVert g\rVert_2 = 1$ for every $g\in\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{G})$;
  \item \emph{degree at most $d$:} $\deg(f)\le d$ for every $f\in\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{F})$ and $\deg(g)\le d$ for every $g\in\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{G})$;
  \item \emph{$\delta$-influences on average:} for every $i\in[N]$,
  \[ \mathop{\mathbb{E}}_{f\leftarrow\mathbf{F}}\bigl[\operatorname{Inf}_i(f)\bigr]\ \le\ \delta(d)
  \qquad\text{and}\qquad
  \mathop{\mathbb{E}}_{g\leftarrow\mathbf{G}}\bigl[\operatorname{Inf}_i(g)\bigr]\ \le\ \delta(d). \]
\end{itemize}
Then there exist $f\in\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{F})$, $g\in\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{G})$, and $x\in\mathcal{Y}^N$ such that
\[ f(x)\cdot g(x)\ \ne\ 0. \]

Note that $\delta$ is allowed to depend on $d$ but not on $N$, and that the group $\mathcal{Y}$ is existentially quantified: exhibiting a single finite abelian group for which the above holds settles the conjecture affirmatively.
